WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.960
We all hear about big demographic
changes underway these days in China, the

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.530
world's most populous country: falling
birth rates, rapid population aging, and

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.279
shrinking workforce totals, with national
population decline just around the

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:21.029
corner. Yet, the most important population
change in China today maybe one we

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:24.660
seldom hear about, one that Chinese
population controllers almost never

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.859
mention. This is the revolution in
Chinese family structure, an astonishing

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:33.180
transformation, a major break with all
past patterns, an unstoppable trend

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.649
driving China into completely new,
uncharted social territory. The vanguard

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:40.320
of this revolution is China's new family
type:

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.399
only children begotten by only children.
This rising group of young men and women

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:49.110
has no siblings, cousins, uncles, or aunts — 
only ancestors, and maybe someday

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.219
descendants. Extended family networks
have been the foundation of social and

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:57.149
economic life in China since Chinese
civilization began. But now these

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.379
networks are atrophying, and for millions
evaporating altogether. Will China

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.520
manage to cope? Or is this a huge
civilization challenging surprise? Stay tuned.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.460
What do you think is China's most
pressing population problem? Let us know

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.450
in our poll. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.520
in 60 seconds, be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.180
from AEI.

